INT. HOTEL BALLROOM, EMPTY - DAWN

A vast chandeliered room stripped after a wedding. Rows of gold chairs stacked in towers. Streamers on the floor. Pale light creeping through tall windows.

CLEMENTINE ASHFORD, 30s, in a rumpled bridesmaid's gown, kicks off her heels and sits on the edge of the stage with a bottle of champagne. HUGO BELMONT, 30s, in a tuxedo with the tie undone, leans on a stack of chairs.

CLEMENTINE
You stayed.

HUGO
I thought someone ought to.

CLEMENTINE
It's six in the morning.

HUGO
Is that what the light means?

CLEMENTINE
It's a very honest light.

HUGO
Then I'll be honest too. I wasn't staying for the wedding.

CLEMENTINE
No?

HUGO
No.

CLEMENTINE
Who were you staying for?

HUGO
The woman who danced with the groom's grandfather for three songs.

CLEMENTINE
He taught me the waltz.

HUGO
He taught you well.

CLEMENTINE
He taught me everything but how to leave a party.

HUGO
You're doing fine.

CLEMENTINE
Am I?

HUGO
You're sitting in a ballroom with a stranger and a bottle.

CLEMENTINE
I wouldn't call you a stranger anymore.

HUGO
What would you call me?

CLEMENTINE
Hugo.

HUGO
That's all?

CLEMENTINE
That's a great deal, Hugo.

A waiter, PROSPER, 20s, wheels in a bussing cart, whistling, and stops dead.

PROSPER
(to the room)
Ma'am, sir. We close the room at six.

HUGO
(to Prosper)
Then close it behind us.

A door at the back opens. VIVIAN BELMONT, 60s, in a dressing gown and curlers, stands in the doorway with a mop.

VIVIAN
(to the room)
Everyone out! The cleaners are coming!

HUGO
Mother.

VIVIAN
(to Hugo)
Hugo, put on your tie.

CLEMENTINE
(to herself)
Of course she's his mother.

VIVIAN
(to Clementine)
And you, my dear, are the girl who sang flat.

CLEMENTINE
I was sharp.

VIVIAN
Beautifully sharp. Sit. I'll bring coffee.

She goes. Hugo and Clementine stare after her. Clementine puts down the bottle.

CLEMENTINE
I think I'm in love with your mother.

HUGO
Everyone is. It's tiresome.
